<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="book-review"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2409511</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6601649</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6601649</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Book Review</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Care of the Terminally Ill Cancer Patient: A Handbook for the Medical Oncologist</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Fallon</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Edinburgh Cancer Center, UK</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>01</day><month>06</month><year>2004</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>26</day><month>05</month><year>2004</year></pub-date><volume>90</volume><issue>11</issue><fpage seq="a">2247</fpage><lpage>2247</lpage><product><bold>MR Owens. Publisher: Charles C Thomas. 2002. ISBN 0398073155 &#x000a3;32</bold></product><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2004 Cancer Research UK</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2004</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research UK</copyright-holder></permissions></article-meta></front><body><p>I found this book very interesting and refreshing. It has added value as a palliative care text because it is written by an oncologist. Michael Owens covers the complete range of issues that the physician may face when caring for a patient with cancer. By definition some of these are only dealt with briefly; however, all are mentioned in a way that is clinically meaningful. Some of the nonphysical problems, for example socioeconomic, are discussed with a strong North American slant but even this area is not without relevance in Europe.</p><p>This text could be very useful for the oncologist in training and even thought provoking for the established oncologist.</p></body></article>